1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:02,200
This is a staircase light.

2
00:00:02,280 --> 00:00:05,230
It comes on before anyone reaches the first step,

3
00:00:05,310 --> 00:00:09,790
because a 24 gigahertz radar in a printed case is watching the approach,

4
00:00:10,020 --> 00:00:13,370
and a four node Node-RED flow is controlling the relay.

5
00:00:13,490 --> 00:00:17,250
That is the whole gadget, and it is not why I made this video.

6
00:00:17,330 --> 00:00:21,490
Over four weeks of evenings, an AI agent wrote the firmware,

7
00:00:21,650 --> 00:00:26,660
the web chart you are looking at, the automation, and the CAD for the enclosure,

8
00:00:26,870 --> 00:00:30,630
driving my own copy of Fusion 360 while I watched.

9
00:00:30,710 --> 00:00:32,960
I was the reviewer and the lab tech.

10
00:00:33,050 --> 00:00:36,700
This is what that looked like, and where the reviewer earned his keep.

11
00:00:38,085 --> 00:00:41,445
The sensor is an Ai-Thinker RD-03D,

12
00:00:41,475 --> 00:00:47,544
a 24 gigahertz frequency-modulated continuous-wave module that tracks up to three targets

13
00:00:47,564 --> 00:00:51,465
and reports position and radial velocity over a serial link,

14
00:00:51,715 --> 00:00:53,245
thirty bytes per frame.

15
00:00:53,345 --> 00:01:00,655
It is wired to a Seeed XIAO ESP32-C6, a thumbnail-sized RISC-V board with WiFi.

16
00:01:00,715 --> 00:01:02,805
The case is two printed parts.

17
00:01:02,885 --> 00:01:06,655
The back plate is eight millimetres thick and carries the radar bay,

18
00:01:06,755 --> 00:01:10,665
posts for the XIAO, five cantilever retention clips,

19
00:01:10,875 --> 00:01:14,795
a USB-C power jack with its capacitor cradled inside,

20
00:01:14,955 --> 00:01:18,355
and six LEGO Technic holes on an eight millimetre pitch,

21
00:01:18,445 --> 00:01:22,245
so the whole thing hangs off a Technic ball joint and can be aimed.

22
00:01:22,355 --> 00:01:27,805
The shell has a thinned radome step over the antennas and a boss that holds the XIAO down.

23
00:01:27,855 --> 00:01:32,775
It printed in PETG on a 0.6 millimetre nozzle with no supports,

24
00:01:32,795 --> 00:01:34,875
after seven revisions of the plate.

25
00:01:36,260 --> 00:01:38,400
Every feature followed the same loop.

26
00:01:38,520 --> 00:01:41,210
I described what I wanted in a paragraph.

27
00:01:41,370 --> 00:01:44,960
The agent wrote a short design spec, I argued with it,

28
00:01:44,980 --> 00:01:48,660
and it wrote a numbered plan and worked through it, testing as it went.

29
00:01:48,770 --> 00:01:52,620
Ten of those spec and plan pairs are in the repository, dated,

30
00:01:52,640 --> 00:01:54,450
and they read like a lab notebook.

31
00:01:54,540 --> 00:01:57,980
The radar frame parser was written as plain C

32
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:00,690
and tested on the Mac before it touched the board,

33
00:02:00,900 --> 00:02:02,777
and the MQTT throttle,

34
00:02:02,797 --> 00:02:06,820
which decides when a target has moved enough to be worth publishing,

35
00:02:06,920 --> 00:02:09,460
is a pure function with its own tests.

36
00:02:09,530 --> 00:02:11,550
Nineteen host tests in all.

37
00:02:11,610 --> 00:02:14,240
Over-the-air updates got a deliberate drill:

38
00:02:14,260 --> 00:02:19,710
we flashed an image built to fail, watched it boot, miss its ninety-second window,

39
00:02:19,810 --> 00:02:22,400
and roll itself back to the previous firmware.

40
00:02:22,500 --> 00:02:25,190
The same safety net nearly became a trap.

41
00:02:25,270 --> 00:02:29,080
The access-point variant would have rolled back after every update,

42
00:02:29,110 --> 00:02:32,480
because the rollback cancel waited for an IP address,

43
00:02:32,550 --> 00:02:36,060
and in access-point mode no IP event ever fires.

44
00:02:36,150 --> 00:02:40,930
The agent caught that while writing the design note, before any code existed.

45
00:02:41,030 --> 00:02:43,630
One line to fix, and nothing to debug.

46
00:02:45,036 --> 00:02:48,336
The enclosure was not designed by generating a mesh.

47
00:02:48,386 --> 00:02:53,486
Autodesk ships a Model Context Protocol server for Fusion 360,

48
00:02:53,586 --> 00:02:57,000
a small local endpoint that lets an agent run Python

49
00:02:57,020 --> 00:03:00,676
against the live Fusion API inside the open document.

50
00:03:00,796 --> 00:03:02,846
The agent wrote a driver for it,

51
00:03:02,866 --> 00:03:05,756
then built the case as a sequence of numbered scripts:

52
00:03:05,996 --> 00:03:12,176
set up parameters, build the back plate, build the shell, place the boards, export.

53
00:03:12,286 --> 00:03:17,036
About nineteen hundred lines of Python, and those scripts are the design.

54
00:03:17,086 --> 00:03:21,376
Every clip, ramp, counterbore and wire notch is a few lines,

55
00:03:21,396 --> 00:03:24,156
with the dimension and the reason side by side.

56
00:03:24,266 --> 00:03:27,046
Here are the crossbars that carry the radar board,

57
00:03:27,066 --> 00:03:29,626
and the comment that records why they moved.

58
00:03:29,716 --> 00:03:31,736
The last script is read-only.

59
00:03:31,786 --> 00:03:35,266
It probes the finished bodies with point-containment queries,

60
00:03:35,326 --> 00:03:38,516
asking Fusion whether there is material at a coordinate,

61
00:03:38,666 --> 00:03:42,746
and asserts that the walls are where the spec says, that the bores are open,

62
00:03:42,896 --> 00:03:45,056
and that nothing roofs a Technic hole.

63
00:03:45,136 --> 00:03:50,906
It caught exactly that, a capacitor rib placed over a pin hole, before I printed it.

64
00:03:51,036 --> 00:03:53,196
A unit test for a mechanical part.

65
00:03:53,316 --> 00:03:55,506
None of this depends on Fusion.

66
00:03:55,566 --> 00:03:59,616
Any CAD package with a scripting API can be driven the same way:

67
00:03:59,876 --> 00:04:06,256
FreeCAD, OpenSCAD, Onshape, or SolidWorks through its automation interface.

68
00:04:07,629 --> 00:04:09,019
Now the honest part:

69
00:04:09,039 --> 00:04:12,789
what went confidently wrong, and how each mistake got caught.

70
00:04:12,969 --> 00:04:15,779
First, the board was resting on its connector.

71
00:04:15,869 --> 00:04:19,579
The agent sized the support bars from the board's overall thickness,

72
00:04:19,599 --> 00:04:22,439
which includes a connector that sticks out the back.

73
00:04:22,569 --> 00:04:26,069
The bare PCB is nearly four millimetres further in,

74
00:04:26,169 --> 00:04:29,659
so as designed the radar sat on the connector and nothing else.

75
00:04:29,729 --> 00:04:31,919
I spotted that in the Fusion model.

76
00:04:31,969 --> 00:04:34,589
The agent then probed the vendor's board model,

77
00:04:34,669 --> 00:04:38,059
found two component-free bands on the rear of the PCB,

78
00:04:38,239 --> 00:04:41,839
and moved the bars to bear there, with the connector hanging free.

79
00:04:41,899 --> 00:04:43,829
Second, the radome gap.

80
00:04:43,909 --> 00:04:48,619
At 24 gigahertz the wavelength, lambda, equals c over f:

81
00:04:48,639 --> 00:04:50,559
twelve and a half millimetres.

82
00:04:50,659 --> 00:04:53,709
The agent had assured me several times that the air gap

83
00:04:53,729 --> 00:04:57,689
from the antenna to the inside of the radome was about a millimetre.

84
00:04:57,759 --> 00:05:01,509
It had measured from the tallest chip, not from the antenna plane.

85
00:05:01,569 --> 00:05:07,799
The true gap was 3.1 millimetres, and a quarter wavelength is 3.125.

86
00:05:07,909 --> 00:05:09,329
At a quarter-wave gap,

87
00:05:09,469 --> 00:05:13,879
the reflection off the radome travels an extra half wavelength out and back,

88
00:05:14,129 --> 00:05:16,419
picks up its phase flip at the plastic,

89
00:05:16,619 --> 00:05:20,369
and arrives back at the antenna in phase with the transmitted wave.

90
00:05:20,499 --> 00:05:22,309
That is the maximum mismatch:

91
00:05:22,329 --> 00:05:25,339
an anti-reflection coating, run backwards.

92
00:05:25,419 --> 00:05:26,549
The rule became:

93
00:05:26,569 --> 00:05:28,939
keep the gap under a tenth of a wavelength.

94
00:05:29,009 --> 00:05:34,169
The radome came down to 1.21 millimetres over both antenna groups,

95
00:05:34,249 --> 00:05:39,729
with 3.1 millimetres of solid plastic in front, and the exterior still flat.

96
00:05:39,849 --> 00:05:43,129
I caught that from a section view, not from the script.

97
00:05:43,209 --> 00:05:46,019
Third, the antenna was going in sideways.

98
00:05:46,119 --> 00:05:47,519
That one was mine:

99
00:05:47,539 --> 00:05:49,099
I had asked for portrait.

100
00:05:49,219 --> 00:05:52,126
The two receive channels that give the module its angle

101
00:05:52,146 --> 00:05:54,759
are spaced across the board's short axis,

102
00:05:54,879 --> 00:06:00,359
about half a wavelength apart, so the wide sixty-degree fan opens across the width,

103
00:06:00,539 --> 00:06:02,749
and the beam is narrow along the length.

104
00:06:02,839 --> 00:06:07,309
For a staircase the fan has to be vertical, so the board mounts landscape.

105
00:06:07,419 --> 00:06:10,309
The agent caught that one from the antenna layout.

106
00:06:10,499 --> 00:06:13,359
Fourth, the printer disagreed with the design.

107
00:06:13,409 --> 00:06:17,479
Walls of 0.6 millimetres on a 0.6 millimetre nozzle.

108
00:06:17,529 --> 00:06:20,669
A tape recess that turned the first layer into a rim.

109
00:06:20,719 --> 00:06:25,579
And later, slender slotted clips with a correct two-percent strain analysis,

110
00:06:25,659 --> 00:06:28,379
which held the board about as well as a wet noodle.

111
00:06:28,429 --> 00:06:29,829
The slots came out.

112
00:06:29,929 --> 00:06:32,949
The clip is now a plain stretch of wall with a lip.

113
00:06:33,029 --> 00:06:34,509
It takes a firm push.

114
00:06:34,589 --> 00:06:35,719
That is fine.

115
00:06:37,129 --> 00:06:40,189
Most of what gets printed in a home shop is a one-off:

116
00:06:40,309 --> 00:06:45,369
a bracket for a sensor, a jig to hold a board while you solder it, a mount,

117
00:06:45,389 --> 00:06:47,509
a box for the thing you just built.

118
00:06:47,569 --> 00:06:50,209
The loop I ran here, describe the part,

119
00:06:50,229 --> 00:06:55,479
have the agent build it parametrically with a probe harness, print it, hold it,

120
00:06:55,689 --> 00:06:57,959
correct it, re-run the script,

121
00:06:58,099 --> 00:07:01,079
is the loop we already run with a human at the keyboard.

122
00:07:01,169 --> 00:07:03,989
Except that the keyboard part now takes minutes,

123
00:07:04,009 --> 00:07:07,239
and leaves a reviewable script and a self-test behind.

124
00:07:07,369 --> 00:07:08,529
Three cautions.

125
00:07:08,609 --> 00:07:13,509
The agent's confidence tells you nothing about its correctness on anything it cannot measure,

126
00:07:13,529 --> 00:07:15,229
and in CAD that is everything.

127
00:07:15,279 --> 00:07:19,209
Every print I did without a section view first, I did twice.

128
00:07:19,309 --> 00:07:21,449
The physics has to come from somewhere,

129
00:07:21,469 --> 00:07:23,829
and the quarter-wave mistake would have shipped.

130
00:07:23,919 --> 00:07:28,509
And treat the scripts, not the live CAD document, as the source of truth,

131
00:07:28,529 --> 00:07:30,059
under version control.

132
00:07:30,139 --> 00:07:34,349
The one time a parameter was tuned in the Fusion window instead of the script,

133
00:07:34,439 --> 00:07:37,529
the next run of the setup script silently reset it.

134
00:07:38,951 --> 00:07:42,311
The light goes off sixty seconds after the last step.

135
00:07:42,381 --> 00:07:46,181
The firmware, the Fusion scripts, the Node-RED flow,

136
00:07:46,221 --> 00:07:49,221
and the dated design notes are all in the repository.

137
00:07:49,291 --> 00:07:52,391
Everything you have seen was synthesized from them:

138
00:07:52,441 --> 00:07:57,361
renders of the actual STL files, a recreation of the live radar chart,

139
00:07:57,381 --> 00:07:58,861
and a synthetic voice.

140
00:07:58,941 --> 00:08:02,061
The agent also wrote this script and built the video.

141
00:08:02,111 --> 00:08:03,771
I reviewed and corrected it.
